Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 04:37:43 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 10861/11171] Record two services failed 18 hours, and a
 journal that keeps nothing

Listing systemd user units while chasing the heartbeat gap turned up two failed
services the hourly dashboard has no way to show.

carnot-arc-daily-prep.service, the ARC-AGI-3 daily submission prep, has been
failed since 2026-09-02 09:44 EDT on exit status 1. That is the November
submission path. arc-news-watch.service has been failed since 09:17 the same
day. Both are daily timers with NRestarts=0.

The second finding is that the cause cannot be recovered. journalctl --user
returns no entries for either unit, nor for carnot-conductor.service, which is
running right now. The conductor's stdout points at a journal socket and
logs/conductor.log has been stale since April, so the live loop's output is
unreadable after the fact and eighteen hours of service failure left no
diagnosable trace.

Deliberately not diagnosed by running the script: prep_daily_submission.py
re-pushes a public Kaggle kernel, which is outward-facing, and Kaggle work is
operator-only. Reading an error message is not a reason to take that action.

Also corrects yesterday's own entry. It recorded py-spy dump as the way to
settle a future heartbeat gap. py-spy is installed but returns Permission
Denied without ptrace privileges, and escalating unasked is not appropriate, so
that remedy is unexecutable as written. Replaces it with one that needs no
privilege: sample /proc/<pid>/wchan, the status State line, and a pgrep -P child
count once a minute across the gap, which separates waiting-on-a-silent-child
from doing-nothing. Today's sample read do_select and S (sleeping), which is
consistent with the former and does not establish it, because the child count
was only seen after the gap closed.

The gap recurred: iteration 13 opened at 07:37:24Z and logged nothing for about
an hour. Two occurrences, still no cause named.

